{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1721","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1721","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1721\",\"parent_revision\":\"6a283f8c4453bff528cdcd8cec287c6e8a94ed993f23022a31b5b3fced3a525e\",\"root_revision\":\"6a283f8c4453bff528cdcd8cec287c6e8a94ed993f23022a31b5b3fced3a525e\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix: find_item silently returns first match on ambiguous title substring\\nDate: 2026-04-12\\nGoal: Fix find_item in parsing.py to raise on ambiguous title substring matches and remove dead-code branch, eliminating silent data-loss when multiple items match a selector.\\nConditions:\\n1. Bug code location at parsing.py:473 with exact code confirmed | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker VERIFIED via Read tool, file:parsing.py:473\\n2. Both defects precisely described (dead-code ternary + silent N>1 match) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker VERIFIED via source code analysis\\n3. Call sites inventory \u2014 11 call sites in operations.py | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Grep tool output confirmed, impact-analyst report confirmed\\n4. Fix design choice \u2014 raise dedicated exception OR plural find_items API, both options in AC | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Acceptance Criteria in item body\\n5. Whether any caller pre-checks match count before calling find_item | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 confirmed zero of 11 call sites pre-check\\n6. Existing test coverage for find_item ambiguous-selector behavior | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 zero tests for ambiguous path in TestFindItem\\n7. Agent instruction file `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` encodes buggy pick-first behavior | Snapshot: (new) \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst scope expansion finding\\n8. server.py MCP error-propagation path for new exception | Snapshot: (new) \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 server.py calls via operations.py, propagation behavior unverified\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by impact-analyst \u2014 zero callers pre-check)\\n- Condition 6: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by impact-analyst \u2014 zero tests for ambiguous path)\\n- Condition 7: (new) AVAILABLE (discovered by impact-analyst \u2014 agent instruction file encodes bug)\\n- Condition 8: (new) DERIVABLE (discovered by impact-analyst \u2014 server.py propagation path)\\nAVAILABLE count: 7\\nDERIVABLE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::find_item` - Defines and owns the selector-resolution contract. The bug lives here (line 473). Fix requires changing the return type or raising a new exception when `len(matches) > 1`. If a dedicated exception is chosen, that exception class must be added to `models.py`. If a plural API is chosen, a new `find_items` function must be created in this same module. Either path changes the public contract of this function. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Public contract change, 11 downstream call sites in `operations.py` will break unless each is updated to handle the new raise/plural path.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` - Must define the new exception class (e.g., `AmbiguousSelectorError(BacklogError)`) if the raise-on-ambiguity approach is taken. Currently defines `BacklogError`, `ItemNotFoundError`, `DuplicateItemError`, `ValidationError`. No `AmbiguousSelector` variant exists. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: New exception class must be added here and exported before `parsing.py` can reference it; omitting it means `parsing.py` must define it locally (split-definition risk).\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::view_item` (line 2758) - Calls `find_item(parse_backlog(), selector)` then falls through to GitHub enrichment. **Does NOT pre-check match count.** If `find_item` raises on ambiguity, this call site will propagate unhandled unless wrapped. The read-only nature limits damage today, but a wrong item being returned is the active bug surface. | Risk: HIGH | Why: No ambiguity guard; wrong item silently returned to callers of `backlog_view`.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::close_item` (lines 2979, 2983) - Calls `find_item` twice (initial + after pull). **Does NOT pre-check match count.** A silently wrong item resolved here causes an irreversible close of the wrong backlog item. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Destructive operation; wrong item resolution causes unrecoverable data loss with no error surfaced.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::resolve_item` (lines 3056, 3060) - Calls `find_item` twice (initial + after pull). **Does NOT pre-check match count.** Same category as `close_item` \u2014 irreversible operation on potentially wrong item. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Destructive operation; permanent resolution of wrong item.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::update_item` (lines 3260, 3264) - Calls `find_item` twice. **Does NOT pre-check match count.** Writes plan, status, or groomed content to the wrong item when selector is ambiguous. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Write corruption; plan or status applied to wrong item, no warning.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::groom_item` (lines 3362, 3389) - Calls `find_item` twice (initial pass + `mark_groomed` re-fetch). **Does NOT pre-check match count.** Groomed content written to wrong item when ambiguous selector used. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Groomed content (including Impact Radius, RT-ICA) written to wrong item.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::strike_entry` (line 3500) - Calls `find_item` once. **Does NOT pre-check match count.** Entry struck from wrong item. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Write operation on wrong item; reversible only if content is backed up.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` (line 3764, pull-single path) - Calls `find_item` in the title-substring branch of `backlog_pull`. **Does NOT pre-check match count.** Pulls content for the wrong item. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Pull from GitHub for wrong item; local cache corrupted with wrong content.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::find_item` docstring - Documents return type as `BacklogItem | None`. If the fix raises an exception, the return type annotation and docstring must be updated to reflect the new contract (`BacklogItem` on unambiguous match, `None` on no match, `AmbiguousSelectorError` on multiple matches). | Risk: LOW | Why: Stale annotation does not cause runtime failures but misleads future readers.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` (line 158) - Lists `find_item()` as a public function under \\\"Item search.\\\" No behavior contract is documented here beyond the name. If the fix adds `find_items()` as a new plural API, this file must list both functions. | Risk: LOW | Why: Architecture doc becomes incomplete if a new function is added and not listed.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_parsing.py::TestFindItem` (lines 448\u2013518) - Contains 8 tests covering: exact title match, partial title match, case-insensitive match, `#N` selector, bare number, no match, unknown issue number, GitHub URL. **Zero tests for the ambiguous case (N>1 title matches).** The ambiguous-selector regression test required by the acceptance criteria does not exist. | Risk: HIGH | Why: No verification exists for the exact behavior being fixed; the entire bug surface is untested.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py` - Multiple tests mock `backlog_core.operations.find_item` directly (lines 632, 653, 738, 759, etc.) to inject specific return values. If `find_item` raises instead of returning `None` or a single item, these mocks are compatible \u2014 they patch the function entirely. However, no operation-level test exercises the ambiguous-selector path through a real item list. Any new exception type must be importable for assertions. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Mock-based tests survive the contract change but provide no coverage for the ambiguous-selector flow end-to-end through `operations.py`.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_groom_mark_groomed.py` - Mocks `find_item` at `backlog_core.operations.find_item`. Same analysis as above \u2014 mock survives contract change but no ambiguous-selector coverage. | Risk: LOW | Why: Isolated mock; not affected by exception type change.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` - Documents the `selector` parameter for `backlog_view`, `backlog_close`, `backlog_resolve`, `backlog_update`, `backlog_groom`, `backlog_pull` as accepting \\\"title substring.\\\" Currently implies silent first-match behavior (no mention of ambiguity error). After the fix, callers expecting silent resolution must learn to use `#N` or more specific substrings. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Skill documentation describes the old contract; agents following it may pass ambiguous substrings and receive unexpected errors.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` (line 15) - **Critically affected.** Flowchart node reads: `\\\"Multiple matches | Pick[Present list to user or pick first when <mode/> is auto]\\\"`. This documents the current silent-first-pick behavior as the intended behavior in auto mode. After the fix, `find_item` will raise on N>1 matches \u2014 the flowchart describing \\\"pick first when auto\\\" will actively mislead agents into expecting silent resolution to succeed. | Risk: HIGH | Why: AI-facing workflow file encodes the buggy behavior as correct; agents following this will not expect or handle the new exception.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` - Multiple entries show selectors passed as title strings (e.g., `selector=\\\"{title}\\\"`). No disambiguation guidance. After the fix, any migration call using a title substring that could match multiple items will raise. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Migration guide instructs title-substring selectors without warning about uniqueness requirement.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` (line 158) - Lists `find_item()` signature in the \\\"Item search\\\" section. If the function raises a new exception, the documentation should reflect that. If `find_items()` is added, it must appear in the export list. | Risk: LOW | Why: Architecture doc will be stale if the function list changes.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` - References `selector: str` in `update_item` and `groom_item` signatures without behavioral commentary on ambiguous selectors. Low urgency \u2014 this is an audit artifact, not instructional. | Risk: LOW | Why: Audit document; does not instruct behavior.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\nNone identified. No CI workflows, YAML configs, or environment variable files reference `find_item` directly. The MCP server loads `operations.py` at startup, which imports `find_item` from `parsing.py` \u2014 a new exception class from `models.py` must be importable at server load time, but no CI configuration change is required.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` - Receives a `selector` parameter described as \\\"either an issue number (`#N`), a bare number, or a title substring.\\\" Instructs calling `backlog_view(selector=selector, summary=False)`. If a title-substring selector is ambiguous, `backlog_view` will now raise an error instead of silently returning the first match. The agent has no current instruction to handle disambiguation errors. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent will encounter an unhandled error if spawned with an ambiguous title-substring selector; instruction must document fallback to `#N` form.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` - Calls `backlog_groom` MCP tool (or `backlog_update` with `groomed_content`) using a selector. If the selector is a title substring, an ambiguous match will now raise. No disambiguation guidance exists in the agent prompt. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Agent writes groomed content via title-substring selector; ambiguous selector will cause a write failure with no recovery instruction.\\n\\n- All agents listed with `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view`, `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom`, `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_update`, `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_close`, `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_resolve` in their tools frontmatter are transitively affected: `plugins/development-harness/agents/fact-checker.md`, `plugins/development-harness/agents/alignment-analyst.md`, `plugins/development-harness/agents/rtica-assessor.md`. These agents use the backlog MCP tools with selectors and may pass title-substring selectors. | Risk: LOW | Why: These agents operate on items already retrieved via `#N` or with fully unique titles in practice, but they have no documented disambiguation protocol.\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` | Role: producer | Connection: defines `find_item` \u2014 the function being fixed | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` | Role: producer | Connection: must define new `AmbiguousSelectorError` exception class | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: 11 call sites of `find_item` across `view_item`, `close_item`, `resolve_item`, `update_item`, `groom_item`, `strike_entry`, pull-single; none pre-check match count | Action: CODE_CHANGE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` | Role: consumer | Connection: calls `operations.py` functions (not `find_item` directly); surfaces errors as MCP tool results; must propagate `AmbiguousSelectorError` as a structured error dict rather than crashing | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_parsing.py` | Role: test | Connection: `TestFindItem` class covers unambiguous cases only; ambiguous case is completely uncovered | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py` | Role: test | Connection: mocks `find_item` to inject return values; mock-based tests survive contract change but no ambiguous-selector end-to-end coverage | Action: TEST_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tests/test_groom_mark_groomed.py` | Role: test | Connection: mocks `find_item`; mock survives contract change | Action: VERIFY_COMPATIBLE | Risk: LOW\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: flowchart node at line 15 explicitly instructs \\\"pick first when auto\\\" for multiple matches \u2014 encodes the buggy behavior as intended | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: HIGH\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/SKILL.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: documents `selector` as accepting \\\"title substring\\\" without disambiguation guidance | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/backlog/CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: shows title-substring selector usage without uniqueness warning | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: receives and passes title-substring selector to `backlog_view`; no disambiguation fallback | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` | Role: agent-instruction | Connection: calls `backlog_groom` with selector; no disambiguation fallback | Action: AGENT_UPDATE | Risk: MEDIUM\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` | Role: documentation | Connection: lists `find_item()` in module export table; will be stale if `find_items()` added | Action: CONTENT_UPDATE | Risk: LOW\\n\\n### Risk Summary\\n\\n- Overall system risk: HIGH\\n- Highest-risk systems:\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` - 11 call sites, none guard against ambiguity; five of them are destructive operations (close, resolve, update, groom, pull) that can corrupt or permanently alter the wrong backlog item\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` - AI-facing workflow file explicitly instructs silent first-pick behavior in auto mode; becomes actively incorrect after the fix and will cause agent misbehavior\\n  - `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_parsing.py` - Zero test coverage for the ambiguous case; the regression test required by acceptance criteria does not exist\\n- Main risk themes:\\n  - compatibility: all 11 `operations.py` call sites must be updated to handle the new exception or plural API\\n  - verification: no test covers ambiguous-selector behavior; fix ships without regression protection unless tests are added\\n  - documentation: AI-facing `find-item.md` encodes the buggy behavior as correct; must be rewritten before agents use the updated server\\n  - behavioral: silent wrong-item resolution in destructive operations (`close_item`, `resolve_item`) represents active data-loss risk beyond the bug description\\n- Scope expansion:\\n  - The `server.py` MCP error-propagation path was not in the original description \u2014 it must verify that `AmbiguousSelectorError` surfaces as a structured error dict rather than an unhandled exception at the MCP boundary.\\n  - The `find-item.md` skill file was not in the original description \u2014 it explicitly encodes the silent-first-pick behavior and must be updated before deployment.\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible (`parsing.py::find_item` fixed, `models.py` new exception class)\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated or verified compatible (all 11 call sites in `operations.py` handle `AmbiguousSelectorError`)\\n- [ ] Every affected test updated or new coverage added (`test_backlog_core_parsing.py::TestFindItem` gains ambiguous-selector test; `test_backlog_core_operations.py` gains end-to-end ambiguous-selector path tests)\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated (`SKILL.md`, `CLI_TO_MCP_MIGRATION.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md` updated)\\n- [ ] Every affected config or CI path updated and validated (None required)\\n- [ ] Every affected agent or skill instruction updated (`find-item.md` rewritten, `impact-analyst.md`, `backlog-item-groomer.md` updated)\\n- [ ] Replacement or migration coverage gaps resolved (N/A \u2014 internal fix, not a migration)\\n- [ ] Hidden transitive dependencies checked (`server.py` MCP error propagation path verified)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n- Ambiguous title substring either raises a dedicated exception listing the candidates, or the selector-resolution API exposes a plural `find_items` that forces the caller to disambiguate.\\n- Dead-code ternary is removed.\\n- Regression test asserts that a substring matching two titles does not silently resolve to one.\\n- All call sites are audited and updated to handle the new contract.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **find_item silently returns first match on ambiguous title substring** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Verified code audit 2026-04-12 \u2014 .tmp/scratch/reports/backlog-mcp-ux-analysis-verified.md (c3)\\n- **Priority**: P0\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-12\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-12)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Open a Python REPL or test script with access to `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py`.\\n2. Create (or ensure existence of) two backlog items whose titles share a common substring \u2014 e.g., titles `\\\"fix auth bug\\\"` and `\\\"fix auth regression\\\"`.\\n3. Call `find_item(items, \\\"fix auth\\\")` where `items` contains both records.\\n4. Observe: the function returns `items[0]` silently \u2014 no exception, no warning, no indication that two matches were found.\\n5. The caller receives a single item and proceeds as if the selector was unambiguous.\\n\\nAlternatively, trigger via any MCP operation that routes through `operations.py` (e.g., `close_item`, `resolve_item`, `update_item`, `groom_item`, `strike_entry`) using a selector substring that matches more than one item title.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\nEffort: Small\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: any caller that passes a non-unique title substring to `find_item` \u2014 11 call sites in `operations.py`, none of which pre-check match count\\n- Destructive operations affected: `close_item`, `resolve_item`, `update_item`, `groom_item`, `strike_entry` \u2014 all can silently mutate or destroy the wrong backlog item\\n- Bottleneck: the single `find_item` function is the sole selector resolution point for all item-targeting MCP operations; no alternative path exists\\n- Agent instructions in `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` encode the buggy \\\"pick first\\\" behavior \u2014 agents following those instructions will perpetuate the ambiguous-selector pattern\\n- Zero existing tests for the ambiguous-selector path means the regression surface is unprotected\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a title substring matches exactly one backlog item, `find_item` returns that item.\\n\\nWhen a title substring matches zero items, `find_item` returns a result that the caller can detect as \\\"not found\\\" (currently `None` \u2014 this behavior is correct and unchanged).\\n\\nWhen a title substring matches more than one item, the caller receives a signal that allows it to distinguish this outcome from both the single-match and zero-match cases. The caller is then able to surface the ambiguity to the user or agent rather than silently proceeding with an arbitrarily selected item.\\n\\nNo destructive operation should proceed on an ambiguous selector without the caller having been informed that multiple matches were found.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Calling `find_item` with a substring that matches N > 1 items produces an outcome that is observably different from the single-match case \u2014 verifiable by reading the return value or catching a raised signal in a test.\\n2. Calling `find_item` with a substring that matches exactly 1 item continues to return that item \u2014 existing single-match behavior is unchanged.\\n3. Calling `find_item` with a substring that matches 0 items continues to return the \\\"not found\\\" sentinel \u2014 existing zero-match behavior is unchanged.\\n4. Each of the 11 call sites in `operations.py` that invoke `find_item` handles the new N > 1 outcome explicitly \u2014 verifiable by grepping for the new signal type and confirming each call site references it.\\n5. At least one automated test exercises the ambiguous-selector path and asserts the observable outcome \u2014 verifiable by running the test suite and seeing the new test pass.\\n6. `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` no longer instructs agents to \\\"pick first\\\" on ambiguous matches \u2014 verifiable by reading the file after the fix.\\n7. No existing passing tests are broken \u2014 verifiable by running the full test suite.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Rule | `/home/user/claude_skills/.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` \u2014 \\\"Branching on Input Values Requires an Explicit Fallback\\\" anti-pattern directly applies |\\n| Rule | `/home/user/claude_skills/.claude/rules/fix-delegation-discipline.md` \u2014 reproduction-first fix cycle |\\n| Source file | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py:473` \u2014 the buggy `find_item` function |\\n| Source file | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 all 11 call sites |\\n| Agent instruction | `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` \u2014 must be updated alongside code fix |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n| File | Role |\\n|------|------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` (line 473) | Contains `find_item` \u2014 the dead-code ternary that must be corrected |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | Contains all 11 call sites; each must handle the new N > 1 signal |\\n| `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` | Encodes the buggy \\\"pick first\\\" behavior in agent instructions \u2014 must reflect correct behavior after fix |\\n| Test files covering `find_item` / `parsing.py` | New test for ambiguous-selector path required; existing tests must remain green |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 self-contained defect in a single function and its call sites\\n- Blocks: any agent or human operation that uses a non-unique title substring as a selector; the `work-backlog-item` skill workflow cannot be trusted to target the correct item until this is resolved\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 the root cause is confined to one function (one ternary expression). The bulk of the work is updating 11 call sites in `operations.py` to handle the new outcome and adding a test for the ambiguous path. Updating the agent instruction file is a documentation edit. No schema changes, no new dependencies, no migration required.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: DIVERGENT\\n**Design intent**: `find_item` should surface ambiguity \u2014 either raise a dedicated exception listing all candidates, or return a typed result that forces the caller to disambiguate. This intent is stated in the item's Acceptance Criteria and is consistent with `.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` \u00a7\\\"Branching on Input Values Requires an Explicit Fallback\\\".\\n**Divergence**: Current implementation at `parsing.py:473` returns `matches[0]` for both the single-match and multiple-match cases. The caller receives a value indistinguishable from a unique match. All 11 call sites in operations.py rely on this behaviour without compensating checks.\\n**Scope note**: `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/find-item.md` instructs agents to pick the first match in auto mode \u2014 this encodes the divergent behaviour as correct and requires update when the fix ships.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n**Rationale**: Traceable failure with an identifiable cause chain \u2014 the dead-code ternary branch in `find_item` means no code path raises or warns on N>1 matches, so all 11 call sites in operations.py silently operate on the wrong item when a title substring is ambiguous.\\n**Analysis Method**: 5-whys\\n**Scenario Target**: ambiguous-selector call on write operation \u2192 `find_item` raises `AmbiguousSelectorError` listing all candidates\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n5-Whys trace:\\n1. Why does find_item return the wrong item? \u2014 Both branches of `return matches[0] if len(matches) == 1 else (matches[0] if matches else None)` return `matches[0]`; the len>1 case has no distinct code path.\\n2. Why does the len>1 case have no distinct path? \u2014 The ternary was written to special-case single vs no match; the multiple-match case was not given its own branch.\\n3. Why was no branch added for multiple matches? \u2014 Selector resolution was written under the implicit assumption that title substrings are unique within a backlog; that assumption was never validated or guarded.\\n4. Why wasn't this caught in review? \u2014 No test for the ambiguous-selector case existed; CI green does not imply uniqueness is enforced.\\n5. Why is this P0 rather than a lower priority? \u2014 Destructive operations (close_item, resolve_item, update_item, groom_item) each call find_item and write to whatever item is returned, with no caller-side ambiguity check. A partial title match can silently close or modify the wrong issue.\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py:450-473` \u2014 the `find_item` selector-resolution function silently returns the first match when a title substring resolves to multiple backlog items:\\n\\n```python\\nmatches = [it for it in items if selector_lower in it.title.lower()]\\nreturn matches[0] if len(matches) == 1 else (matches[0] if matches else None)\\n```\\n\\nTwo defects in two lines:\\n\\n1. **Dead-code branch** \u2014 both `len(matches) == 1` and `len(matches) > 1` return `matches[0]`. The ternary is structurally redundant; the intent (special-case single match) is not actually encoded.\\n2. **Silent ambiguity** \u2014 when a caller passes a title substring that matches N>1 items, the function returns whichever item happens to sort first, with no error, no warning, no indication to the caller that the selector was ambiguous. This is the exact \\\"Branching on Input Values Requires an Explicit Fallback\\\" anti-pattern from `.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Observed Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- An agent that calls `backlog_view(selector=\\\"auth\\\")` against a backlog with both \\\"auth-refresh-flow\\\" and \\\"auth-session-cleanup\\\" gets one item back without knowing the other exists.\\n- Writes that resolve via title substring (groom, update, close) can land on the wrong item.\\n- Callers currently have no protocol for exhaustive-match disambiguation because the API pretends the selector was unique.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Open Question\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nWhether every current call site compensates by pre-checking match count before calling `find_item` has not been verified. If even one caller does not, this is an active bug rather than a latent one.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `.tmp/scratch/reports/backlog-mcp-ux-analysis-verified.md` \u00a7c3 (verified via Grep in session)\\n- `.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` \u2192 \\\"Branching on Input Values Requires an Explicit Fallback\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 1 Find Item Silently Returns First Match On Ambiguous Title Substring\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**verdict**: VERIFIED\\n\\n**evidence**: \\nSource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py:473`\\nRetrieved: 2026-04-12\\n\\nThe function returns: `return matches[0] if len(matches) == 1 else (matches[0] if matches else None)`\\n\\nWhen matches list has 2+ items (N>1), `len(matches) == 1` evaluates to False, so the ternary short-circuits to the else branch: `(matches[0] if matches else None)`. Since a non-empty list is truthy, this returns `matches[0]` \u2014 the first item.\\n\\nTest confirmation: Calling with matches=[1,2] returns 1; matches=[1,2,3] returns 1. No warning or error is raised.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 2 Code Contains Dead Code Branch Both Len 1 And Len 1 Return Matches 0\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**verdict**: VERIFIED\\n\\n**evidence**:\\nSource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py:473`\\nRetrieved: 2026-04-12\\n\\nCode analysis:\\n- When `len(matches) == 1`: first ternary branch executes \u2192 returns `matches[0]`\\n- When `len(matches) > 1`: condition False, falls to else \u2192 evaluates `(matches[0] if matches else None)` \u2192 returns `matches[0]`\\n- When `len(matches) == 0`: condition False, else branch \u2192 evaluates falsy matches \u2192 returns `None`\\n\\nBoth the True (len==1) and False/len>1 branches execute the same operation when matches is non-empty: return `matches[0]`. The dead-code pattern is a redundant nested ternary that produces identical behavior.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 3 Function Returns One Item With No Error Warning Or Indication Of Ambiguity\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**verdict**: VERIFIED\\n\\n**evidence**:\\nSource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py:450-473`\\nRetrieved: 2026-04-12\\n\\nThe function signature and implementation:\\n```python\\ndef find_item(items: list[BacklogItem], selector: str) -> BacklogItem | None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Find item by title substring, #N, bare number, or GitHub issue URL.\\n    ...\\n    Returns:\\n        Matching BacklogItem or None.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    ...\\n    selector_lower = selector.lower()\\n    matches = [it for it in items if selector_lower in it.title.lower()]\\n    return matches[0] if len(matches) == 1 else (matches[0] if matches else None)\\n```\\n\\nNo logging, no exception, no warning. Returns a single BacklogItem (or None). Callers cannot detect that matches contained multiple items.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 4 Evidence Document Tmp Scratch Reports Backlog Mcp Ux Analysis Verified Md Exists\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**verdict**: REFUTED\\n\\n**evidence**:\\nSource: File system search 2026-04-12\\n\\nCommand: `find /home/user/claude_skills -name \\\"*backlog-mcp-ux*\\\" -o -name \\\"*ux-analysis*\\\"`\\nResult: (no output \u2014 file does not exist)\\n\\nThe document referenced in Claim 4 does not exist. No such analysis artifact was found.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Claim 5 Claude Rules Silent Failure Prevention Md Contains Branching On Input Values Requires An Explicit Fallback\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**verdict**: VERIFIED\\n\\n**evidence**:\\nSource: `/home/user/claude_skills/.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` lines 40-66\\nRetrieved: 2026-04-12\\n\\nThe document contains the exact section title and explains:\\n> Every `if`/`elif` chain or match on an input value must have a final branch that either acts or errors. Falling through to `return unchanged` without logging is a silent data loss bug.\\n\\nThe `find_item` function violates this rule: when multiple matches exist, it silently returns the first match without indicating ambiguity or logging the situation. No branch explicitly handles the N>1 case with warning or error.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"83e2575bc64954e87f180fc5093653a6d9d3ce35370c2f762d394ac2dc70f7c4\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCxH0w\"}"}